Relevant columns and why they matter  
• event_date : cohort definition (first engagement date) and date arithmetic for the “second week” (day‑8 → day‑14)  
• event_name : distinguishes the two kinds of events we need – the various quick‑play events and the retention signal session_start  
• user_pseudo_id : stable user identifier used to build cohorts and measure returns  
• event_params : holds the quick‑play subtype (e.g. quickplay_type, mode, or map) that we must compare; we pull it by UNNEST‑ing and filtering on key = 'quickplay_type' (or any other key we discover)  

Typical filters / join conditions  
• event_name LIKE '%quickplay%'                               –– identify all quick‑play events  
• event_name  = 'session_start'                               –– retention signal  
• _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20180801' AND '20180815'             –– users who FIRST engaged 1‑15 Aug 2018  
• event_date  BETWEEN '20180809' AND '20180829'               –– second‑week window (user‑specific once we know the cohort start)  
• PARSE_DATE('%Y%m%d', event_date) - PARSE_DATE('%Y%m%d', first_play_date) BETWEEN 8 AND 14  

Below are ten progressively richer inspection / analysis queries (all capped with LIMIT 20).  
Replace <PROJECT>.<DATASET> with firebase-public-project.analytics_153293282 in your console if you copy‑paste.  

```sql
-- 1.  All distinct event names that contain the word “quickplay” (Aug‑01 – Aug‑15)
